The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
xfarray_destroy(ragi->iunlink_prev);
xfarray_destroy(ragi->iunlink_next);
```
Reason: These lines unconditionally invoke `xfarray_destroy` on pointers that may be NULL if the corresponding data structures were never allocated (e.g., due to early errors or interrupted setup). This can cause NULL pointer dereferences or invalid memory accesses.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (ragi->iunlink_prev)
    xfarray_destroy(ragi->iunlink_prev);
ragi->iunlink_prev = NULL;
if (ragi->iunlink_next)
    xfarray_destroy(ragi->iunlink_next);
ragi->iunlink_next = NULL;
```
Reason: The added null checks ensure that `xfarray_destroy` is only called when the pointer is valid, preventing dereference of NULL. Setting the pointers to NULL after destruction ensures safe cleanup and avoids double-free or use-after-free issues.

